### 2. HALBACH ARRAY FIELD MODEL

#### 2.1 Halbach magnetization structure

A linear Halbach array with N pole pairs and spatial period λ (pole pitch τ = λ/2) produces a one-sided intensified field. The magnetization vector for the n-th magnet segment rotates by π/2 per segment, creating constructive interference on the working face and near-cancellation on the back face. For an ideal infinite planar Halbach array, the normal component of B at the plate face (air gap g above the array surface) is:

B_z(x, g) = B_r · (1 − e^{−k·h_m}) · e^{−k·g} · sin(kx)

where:
B_r = remanent flux density of the magnet material (T)
h_m = magnet height (m)
k = 2π/λ = wavenumber
g = air gap between magnet face and conductive plate (m)
x = position along the array

For a finite array of N_p pole pairs, the field is modulated by an aperture factor:

B_z(x, g) = B_r · (1 − e^{−k·h_m}) · e^{−k·g} · (2/π) · Σ_{n=1,3,5,...} (1/n) · sin(nπ/N_p) · sin(nkx)

The dominant fundamental term gives the peak field strength at the plate surface (g = 0):

B₀ = B_r · (1 − e^{−k·h_m})

For NdFeB N52 grade: B_r = 1.48 T. With h_m = 20 mm and λ = 50 mm (k = 125.7 rad/m):

B₀ = 1.48 × (1 − e^{−125.7 × 0.020}) = 1.48 × (1 − e^{−2.514}) = 1.48 × 0.919 = 1.36 T

This is a very strong field achievable with compact geometry.

#### 2.2 Back-iron enhancement

Adding a soft-iron back-plate (μ_r ≈ 1000–4000) behind the magnet array channels flux that would otherwise escape into the back side, increasing B₀ by a factor of κ_bi:

κ_bi ≈ 1 + (μ_r − 1) · (h_bi / (h_bi + h_m))

For h_bi = 5 mm iron backing: κ_bi ≈ 1.20–1.45, so effective B₀ can reach 1.6–1.9 T in practice. Literature confirms that inclusion of back iron can increase damping coefficients by a factor of up to 5 in eddy-current damper configurations compared to configurations without it. [ASCE Library](https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/%28ASCE%29AS.1943-5525.0000891)

#### 2.3 Dual-array configuration

To maximize force without increasing overall displacement, the EOPL uses a sandwich configuration: one Halbach array above and one below the copper plate, both oriented with their strong-field faces toward the plate. The total damping force doubles to first order (assuming negligible cross-coupling between the two arrays):

F_d^{total} = 2 · C_em · v = −2 · C_em · ẋ

The effective field at the plate midpoint from both arrays (gap g/2 each) is:

B_eff = 2 · B₀ · e^{−k · (g/2)} · (1 − e^{−k·h_m})

This becomes the design-driving parameter.

