### 3. FULL ANALYTICAL DAMPING MODEL

#### 3.1 Damping coefficient derivation — thin-plate limit

For a rectangular plate (dimensions: width w along y, length l along x, thickness d) moving in the x-direction with velocity v through a field with spatial spectrum B_z(k_x, k_y), the power dissipated is:

P_diss = (σ · d / 2) · ∫∫ |v · B_z(k_x, k_y)|² · |H(k_x, k_y)|² dk_x dk_y

where H(k, ·) is a geometry factor. For the dominant fundamental harmonic of a Halbach array (treating the field as B_z = B₀ sin(k_x · x)):

P_diss = (1/2) · σ · d · w · l · (v · B₀)² · F_correction(k, d, σ, v)

where F_correction accounts for finite plate geometry and skin effects. The damping coefficient is C_em = 2P_diss / v², giving:

C_em = σ · d · w · l · B₀² · F_correction

In the low-velocity, thin-plate limit (k·d → 0, Rm → 0):

F_correction → F_geom = (1 − sinc(k·l)) · (correction for width w)

For k·l ≫ 1 (plate longer than one spatial period), F_correction → 1 and:

C_em ≈ σ · d · w · l · B₀²

#### 3.2 Numerical estimates for EOPL baseline design

Plate material: copper (σ = 5.8 × 10⁷ S/m)
Plate dimensions: w = 0.30 m, l = 0.25 m, d = 0.004 m
B₀ (dual Halbach, effective) = 1.5 T (achievable as computed above)
Back-iron enhancement factor: 1.3

C_em = 5.8 × 10⁷ × 0.004 × 0.30 × 0.25 × 1.5² × 1.3
= 5.8 × 10⁷ × 0.004 × 0.075 × 2.25 × 1.3
= 5.8 × 10⁷ × 8.775 × 10⁻⁴
≈ 5.09 × 10⁴ N·s/m ≈ 50.9 kN·s/m

This is a very large damping coefficient. At a relative seat velocity of v = 2 m/s (typical early-phase collision seat motion), the damping force is:

F_d = C_em · v = 50,900 × 2 = 101,800 N ≈ 102 kN

For an occupant mass m = 80 kg, the deceleration contribution from the EM damper alone would be:

a_em = F_d / m = 101,800 / 80 ≈ 1272 m/s² ≈ 130 g

This is far too high for isolated application; however, the EOPL is not a standalone system. The damper operates in parallel with the seat structure compliance and over the full collision duration (~60–100 ms), meaning the velocity v is at its peak only for a brief instant. The key metric is the integral of the force over displacement (the energy absorbed), not the instantaneous peak.

Energy absorbed per unit displacement:

dE/dx = F_d = C_em · v(x)

Total energy absorbed over seat travel s_max (stroke limit ≈ 80 mm):

E_abs = ∫₀^{s_max} C_em · v(x) dx = C_em · ∫₀^{s_max} v dx

For a uniformly decelerating plate from v₀ = 2 m/s to 0 over s_max = 0.08 m:

E_abs = C_em · (v₀² / (2 · a_decel)) ... integrated → C_em · v₀² · s_max / (2v₀) = C_em · v₀ · s_max / 2
= 50,900 × 2 × 0.04 ≈ 4072 J per stroke per plate pair

For a 56 km/h frontal impact (the standard NCAP test), total occupant kinetic energy is:

E_occ = (1/2) · m · v² = (1/2) · 80 · (15.6)² ≈ 9,734 J

The EM module absorbs ~4 kJ per stroke, representing approximately 40% of the occupant's kinetic energy — a meaningful fraction, enabling the remaining energy to be distributed over a longer time span, directly lowering peak deceleration.

#### 3.3 Stroke optimization and displacement limit

The maximum permissible seat displacement without occupant-to-vehicle contact (steering wheel, dashboard) constrains the stroke. Accounting for seatbelt play (~15 mm) and crash structure compression (~100 mm), available stroke for the EM seat module is s_max ≈ 60–80 mm. The work-energy theorem requires:

F_avg = E_abs / s_max

For E_abs = 4 kJ and s_max = 0.075 m: F_avg = 53,333 N

This maps to 66 g average deceleration for an 80 kg occupant — comparable to existing seatbelt loading levels, confirming the system operates in a physically realistic force regime.

