### 4. BIOMECHANICAL PERFORMANCE TARGETS

#### 4.1 Injury criteria and thresholds

The following regulatory and biomechanical thresholds govern EOPL design targets:

Head Injury Criterion (HIC-15):

HIC = max_{t1,t2} [(1/(t2−t1)) · ∫_{t1}^{t2} a(t)dt]^2.5 · (t2−t1)

This criterion is computed over time intervals t1 and t2 (in seconds), with the duration (t2−t1) limited to a maximum of 36 ms and preferably 15 ms for the HIC-15 metric. The HIC scale ranges from 0 to 1600, with 1600 corresponding to mortal injuries and approximately 700 corresponding to the severe injury threshold. [uspto](https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/12509014)

Chest deceleration (3 ms clip): a_chest ≤ 60 g (regulatory), ≤ 40 g (EOPL target)

Neck injury criterion (Nij): composite of tension, compression, flexion, extension forces; target Nij ≤ 1.0

Chest deflection: ≤ 50 mm (regulatory), ≤ 35 mm (EOPL target)

Occupant contact velocity (OCV): The velocity at which the occupant contacts interior surfaces; target OCV ≤ 6 m/s (from ≥ 10 m/s in unrestrained crash)

In a typical 56 km/h frontal impact without electromagnetic assistance, the restrained occupant remains in the moderate injury zone (AIS 2–3) with chest deflection approximately 45–55 mm and typically 2–4 fractured ribs. [Firgelliauto](https://www.firgelliauto.com/blogs/engineering-calculators/car-crash-force-calculator) The EOPL targets reduction to AIS 1–2 through peak deceleration reduction.

#### 4.2 Optimal deceleration waveform

The total deceleration (time-integrated deceleration) experienced by the occupant during a crash is fixed for a given crash severity. Therefore, the optimal waveform for minimizing occupant injury should consist of an initial interval producing a large deceleration upon crash detection, an intermediate interval producing an opposite deceleration, and a final interval producing average deceleration — allowing the deceleration to be spread over an extended period. [uspto](https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/6843504)

The EOPL contributes to shaping this waveform by front-loading energy absorption (large C_em immediately post-impact) and allowing the seat to decelerate the occupant more gradually thereafter. The control target is a constant-jerk deceleration profile:

a(t) = a₀ + J · t, where J = da/dt = const

This distributes the deceleration impulse uniformly, avoiding the sharp spike that maximizes HIC. The EM damper achieves this through adaptive field control (see Section 6).

#### 4.3 Jerk limit

Biomechanical research establishes that jerk (rate of change of acceleration) drives soft-tissue injury independent of peak acceleration. The EOPL target is:

|J| = |da/dt| ≤ 500 g/s (compared to ≥ 5000 g/s in uncontrolled impacts)

This requires that the EM damping force rise smoothly rather than stepwise — a natural property of eddy-current systems (F_d = C_em · v, which rises at rate dF_d/dt = C_em · a, proportional to acceleration rather than with an instantaneous discontinuity).

