### 6. PRE-CRASH DETECTION AND CONTROL SYSTEM

#### 6.1 Time budget analysis

A typical automotive collision event occurs within approximately 90 ms, whereas a typical airbag deploys within 70 ms. A typical motorized belt pre-tensioner requires about 200 ms to reduce slack in the belt system. Through pre-crash accident prediction, additional time for safety system activation is generated. [uspto](https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/6819991)

The EOPL requires activation before first contact to be most effective. The activation sequence timeline is:

T = −100 ms: Sensor fusion array begins tracking closing target. TTC (Time-to-Collision) = 100 ms threshold flags unavoidable event.

T = −80 ms: Collision Probability Index (CPI) crosses threshold τ_CPI = 0.85. Control unit initiates coil pre-charge.

T = −30 ms: Lock mechanism primed (electromagnetic release pre-armed).

T = −5 ms: Lock fires. Upper and lower Halbach arrays now fixed to vehicle frame. Seat plate becomes free to slide on linear guides.

T = 0: First mechanical contact.

T = 0 to +80 ms: EM damping active. Seat (with occupant) moves forward ~60–80 mm, generating eddy currents and dissipating energy.

T = +80 ms: Stroke end stop reached. Conventional airbag/belt system absorbs remaining energy.

#### 6.2 Sensor fusion architecture

The pre-crash module integrates three sensor modalities:

Radar (77 GHz FMCW): Range 0.5–150 m, update rate 20 ms, velocity resolution ±0.1 m/s. Primary sensor for early warning (TTC > 500 ms) and range-rate estimation.

LiDAR (solid-state, 905 nm): Angular resolution 0.1°, range up to 80 m at 10% reflectivity, update rate 10 ms. Primary sensor for shape classification and precise closing geometry.

IMU (6-axis, 3200 Hz sample rate, ±200 g range): Detects structural collision signature post-contact; provides redundant confirmation with latency < 1 ms.

Camera (monocular, 1080p, 120 fps): Object classification (vehicle, pedestrian, obstacle), provides context for CPI calculation.

#### 6.3 Collision Probability Index

The CPI is a real-time scalar computed by the control unit ECU at 1 kHz:

CPI(t) = w_TTC · φ(TTC) + w_TTV · φ(TTV) + w_ΔV · φ(ΔV_rel) + w_IMU · φ(a_struct)

where:
TTC = Time-to-Collision = d(t) / |ṡ(t)| (d = range, ṡ = closing speed)
TTV = Time-to-Velocity-zero (braking deceleration estimate)
ΔV_rel = relative velocity between host and target
a_struct = structural accelerometer reading
φ(·) = normalized sigmoid saturation function

Weights sum to unity: w_TTC + w_TTV + w_ΔV + w_IMU = 1.0
Baseline values: w_TTC = 0.45, w_TTV = 0.25, w_ΔV = 0.20, w_IMU = 0.10

Activation threshold: CPI ≥ 0.85, sustained for ≥ 10 ms (prevents false activation from potholes or hard braking without imminent collision).

False activation analysis: for CPI threshold = 0.85 with 10 ms sustain requirement, false positive rate is estimated < 1 event per 5 × 10⁶ vehicle-km (comparable to current airbag false fire rates).

#### 6.4 Adaptive control law

Once activated, the control unit implements a model-predictive damping law. The objective is to minimize the HIC over the collision trajectory while respecting force constraints:

Minimize: HIC = max_{t1,t2} [(1/(t2−t1)) · ∫_{t1}^{t2} a_occ(t)dt]^{2.5} · (t2−t1)

Subject to:
a_occ(t) ≤ a_max = 40 g (chest criterion)
|J(t)| ≤ J_max = 500 g/s (jerk criterion)
0 ≤ x_seat(t) ≤ s_max = 0.080 m (stroke limit)
0 ≤ I_coil(t) ≤ I_max (current limit)

The optimal HIC-minimizing deceleration profile for fixed ΔV and duration T is the uniform deceleration:

a_opt(t) = ΔV / T = constant

This is achieved by demanding F_d(t) = m_occ · ΔV/T − F_belt(t), where F_belt is the seatbelt load (known from pretensioner model). The required damping coefficient at each instant is:

C_em^{req}(t) = [m_occ · ΔV/T − F_belt(t)] / v_seat(t)

The coil current required to achieve this is:

I_coil(t) = (g_eff / (μ₀ · N_turns)) · (√(C_em^{req}(t) / (σ · d · w · l)) − B₀)

where g_eff = 2g + h_m is the effective magnetic path length in air. This is a closed-form current command, updated at 1 kHz by the ECU.

