### 7. POWER AND ENERGY SUBSYSTEM

#### 7.1 Capacitor bank specification

The adaptive coil bank requires rapid energy delivery. Coil inductance per bank: L_coil ≈ 8 mH (estimated from N_turns, geometry). Peak coil current: I_peak = 50 A. Energy per coil bank:

E_coil = (1/2) · L_coil · I_peak² = 0.5 × 8 × 10⁻³ × 2500 = 10 J

For eight coil banks: E_total = 80 J

Ultracapacitor bank specification:
Capacitance: C_UC = 100 F
Operating voltage: V_UC = 16 V (commercially available modules)
Stored energy: E_UC = (1/2) · C · V² = 0.5 × 100 × 256 = 12,800 J

This provides more than adequate energy reserve for a single collision event, with a large safety margin. The capacitor bank mass is approximately 0.8 kg (based on Maxwell Technologies BCAP0100 specifications).

#### 7.2 Normal-operation energy harvest

The passive Halbach array configuration (arrays locked to seat, no relative motion) does not harvest energy in normal driving. However, an optional regenerative mode can be implemented by partially releasing the lock during normal suspension travel. The suspension stroke amplitude is typically 50–100 mm at 1–5 Hz; the harvestable power is small (< 5 W) but sufficient to maintain capacitor charge state over long trips. This is a non-critical design feature.

#### 7.3 Eddy-current energy recovery

Research demonstrates that eddy current damper designs capable of guiding induced currents out through external windings can convert part of the impact energy into electrical energy, with single-impact energy conversion efficiency reaching approximately 2.33%. [Extrica](https://www.extrica.com/article/21672) For the EOPL, this feature is secondary (the primary function is dissipation, not recovery), but could be incorporated into future generations for partial capacitor recharge post-minor-impact.

