### 10. OPEN RESEARCH QUESTIONS AND KNOWN LIMITATIONS

The linear damping assumption (F_d ∝ v) breaks down at high velocities where the magnetic Reynolds number Rm = μ₀ σ v L approaches 1. The linear damping approximation is only valid for a limited range of low velocity; the velocity range of linearity can be extended by reducing the thickness or conductivity of the conductive plate. [ASCE Library](https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/%28ASCE%29AS.1943-5525.0000891) For crash velocities exceeding ~5 m/s (relative seat velocity), the full nonlinear model must be used for accurate prediction. The EOPL control law must therefore use the full nonlinear C_em(v) relation rather than the simplified linear form.

The 20 kg added mass is a primary engineering challenge. Path to reduction: replace solid copper plate with a copper-coated carbon fiber substrate (30% mass reduction), use thin-magnet high-energy-product material (SmCo2:17 at B_r = 1.12 T but at 60% of NdFeB mass density), and reduce array footprint to 250 mm × 200 mm.

The lock-release mechanism must function correctly within 5 ms. An electromagnetic solenoid lock release has been demonstrated at < 3 ms actuation time in automotive pre-tensioner applications; a pyrotechnic pin alternative achieves < 1 ms but is single-use. The baseline design specifies a solenoid release for repeatability, with pyrotechnic backup in the severe-collision mode.

Side-impact and rollover scenarios present different seat relative velocity directions, requiring a multi-axis guide system or direction-selective electromagnetic geometry. The current design addresses frontal impact only; a second-generation implementation would add lateral guides for side-impact.

Interaction with occupant mass variability (40 kg child to 130 kg adult) requires the control law to estimate occupant mass in real time from seat pressure sensors and adjust the coil current command accordingly. This is a standard automotive control problem with established solutions (seat occupant classification systems already exist for airbag suppression).

