### CONCLUSION

The EOPL is physically grounded in well-established electromagnetic damping theory, with quantitative estimates indicating that a seat-integrated Halbach-array eddy-current module can absorb approximately 35–40% of the occupant's kinetic energy in a 56 km/h frontal impact, targeting a peak chest deceleration reduction from ~58 g to ≤ 38 g and HIC-15 reduction from ~700 to ≤ 400. The system requires a total added mass of approximately 20 kg (reducible to ~14 kg with materials optimization), a pre-crash activation latency of 5 ms, and a capacitor energy reserve of ~12.8 kJ. The adaptive control law is closed-form at 1 kHz update rate, computing optimal coil current from real-time occupant mass, seat velocity, and seatbelt load estimates. Major unresolved challenges are mass reduction, multi-axis protection, and lock-release reliability qualification, each of which represents a well-defined engineering problem amenable to systematic experimental investigation.

